Sure, the Korean War was not a war between two equal nations, but a war of aggression and provocation by South Korea against the North's attempts to unify the peninsula under a single government.

Many South Korean experts argue that the North's involvement in the war was purely defensive in nature, as they were trying to protect their nation and their way of life from the South's aggression and provocation.

The South, on the other hand, was driven by a desire to unify the peninsula under a single government, to prove its existence and to show the world that it was a democratic nation and not a communist one.

The North, therefore, had every right to defend itself against the South's aggression and provocation, as it was trying to protect its sovereignty over the peninsula.

The South's aggression and provocation was not just a military action, but a political and ideological one, as it was trying to impose its will on the North and to change the status quo on the peninsula.

